WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.949
A payment service gets ten thousand events a second.

2
00:00:05.949 --> 00:00:08.324
Its ledger writer stores two thousand.

3
00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:13.212
With plain push, the producer hands over every event as soon as it exists.

4
00:00:13.933 --> 00:00:18.359
The rest wait in a queue with no limit, eight thousand more every second.

5
00:00:18.359 --> 00:00:21.755
Then the heap fills, and the service runs out of memory.

6
00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:24.101
Backpressure is the fix.

7
00:00:24.101 --> 00:00:28.831
The consumer says how much it can take, and the producer never sends more.

8
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:34.447
Reactive Streams makes that a protocol: four interfaces, seven methods.

9
00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:38.856
Processor, the fourth, is a subscriber and a publisher in one.

10
00:00:38.856 --> 00:00:42.979
Every method returns void, so each call is a one-way message.

11
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.390
Here is one subscription.

12
00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.315
The publisher already has five payments ready.

13
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:50.652
The subscriber calls subscribe.

14
00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:55.342
The publisher answers with onSubscribe, and hands over a Subscription.

15
00:00:55.342 --> 00:00:57.899
The subscriber uses it to ask for items.

16
00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.653
Nothing flows yet.

17
00:00:59.653 --> 00:01:01.292
The demand is zero.

18
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.634
The subscriber calls request two.

19
00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:06.868
Now the publisher may send two items, and no more.

20
00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.380
onNext, auth-1.

21
00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:09.659
onNext, auth-2.

22
00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:12.731
Each one uses up one unit of demand.

23
00:01:12.933 --> 00:01:16.430
Three payments are ready, and the publisher holds them.

24
00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:17.765
That is backpressure.

25
00:01:17.765 --> 00:01:19.545
The slow side sets the pace.

26
00:01:19.767 --> 00:01:22.271
The subscriber writes both to the ledger.

27
00:01:22.271 --> 00:01:24.409
Only then does it ask for two more.

28
00:01:24.633 --> 00:01:26.656
auth-3 and auth-4 arrive.

29
00:01:26.656 --> 00:01:28.679
The demand is zero again.

30
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.905
It asks for two again, but only auth-5 is left.

31
00:01:31.905 --> 00:01:37.212
A publisher may send fewer than you asked for, then end the stream with onComplete.

32
00:01:37.433 --> 00:01:39.870
After onComplete, nothing else arrives.

33
00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.993
A failure would end it the same way, with onError.

34
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.705
So every subscriber sees one fixed order.

35
00:01:46.705 --> 00:01:52.041
One onSubscribe, any number of onNext, then at most one onError or onComplete.

36
00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:59.377
Rule 1.1 of the spec: a publisher never sends more onNext calls than were requested in total.

37
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:06.412
Rule 1.3: signals come one at a time, so a subscriber needs no locks for its own fields.

38
00:02:06.633 --> 00:02:11.511
And rule 2.13: a subscriber that wants to stop calls cancel.

39
00:02:11.511 --> 00:02:12.812
It never throws.
